import hooks from "eslint-plugin-react-hooks";

const linter = new Linter();

for (const expression of [
  "Date.now()",
  "new Date().getTime()",
  "new Date().getFullYear()",
]) {
  const messages = linter.verify(
    `function Component() { return <div>{${expression}}</div>; }`,
    [{
      plugins: { "react-hooks": hooks },
      rules: { "react-hooks/purity": "error" },
      languageOptions: {
        ecmaVersion: 2022,
        sourceType: "module",
        parserOptions: { ecmaFeatures: { jsx: true } },
      },
    }],
  );

  console.log(expression, messages.map(({ ruleId }) => ruleId));
}
```

Actual output:

```text
Date.now() [ 'react-hooks/purity' ]
new Date().getTime() []
new Date().getFullYear() []
```

### Expected behavior

All three expressions should report an impure clock read during render. Their results can change without any change to props, state, or context.

This concerns the zero-argument constructor. Constructing a date from an explicit, stable timestamp should remain allowed.

